entityDescription:
The aim of the Foundation is to develop and test innovative wildlife management methods and technologies and to make proposals on upscaling methodologies and financing mechanisms for those forms of wildlife management. 
The Foundation seeks to achieve among other things:
a. organizing international workshops and conferences with key players in wildlife management, credit, finance, accountancy and other relevant knowledge fields;
b. setting up a platform where wildlife managers and experts can meet and communicate, and share knowledge with each other;
c. the development of performance parameters focusing on finances, health assessments, reproductive success, mortality, population sizes and successful functioning of wildlife in situ;
d. the development of an adequate form of data management and analysis to share the results of wildlife management interventions between wildlife managers and experts;
e. the development and commissioning (by local parties) of field tests, including in Asia and Africa;
f. the development of new methods and technologies for wildlife management and their testing and application, including the establishment of an independent monitoring, verification and reporting system of the intervention results;
g. initiating and, if necessary, facilitating scaling-up plans if applied interventions prove effective; 
h. identifying global wildlife management training institutes where the developed innovative forms of --wildlife management can be included in the curriculum; and
i. exploring alternatives to philanthropic funding for protecting wildlife and their habitats, including how these might be structured.

